# Rational Design of Coordination Bond Connected Metal Organic Frameworks/MXene Hybrids for Efficient Solar Water Splitting

## abstract
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted great interest in photocatalysis, but their activity is hampered by the issue of severe carrier recombination. Here, through a carboxyl group assisted coordination route, MXene decorated with carboxyl groups provides chelation sites enabling coordination with UiO‐66‐NH2(Zr/Ti) (UZT) to fabricate a tightly connected UiO‐66‐NH2(Zr/Ti)/carboxyl‐functionalized MXene (UZT/CFMX) heterostructure. This is the first instance of direct chemical bonding of MOFs‐involved heterostructure via a coordination bond. The critical role of decorated carboxyl groups can be determined so that 1) these can help to establish a strong coordination bond between two materials; 2) act as bridge to promote the electrons transfer from MOFs to MXene, thus relieving carrier recombination, and 3) most interestingly, the carbon atom on the carboxyl group forms a bond with the oxygen from water stimulating the water to dissociate into OH* and H*, thus adding additional reaction pathways for promoting photocatalytic water splitting. Accordingly, the resulting UZT/CFMX shows efficient solar‐driven photocatalytic performance for water splitting. The H2 evolution rate is as high as 2187 µmol g−1 h−1, 20 times higher than that of UZT and 4 times higher than that of UiO‐66‐NH2 (Zr/Ti)/MXene (UZT/MX), also surpassing the majority of reported MOF‐based photocatalysts.

## 1. Introduction
Recently, photocatalytic water splitting has been considered as a potential and cost-effective technology for large-scale production of hydrogen from renewable solar energy.[1–5] Hence, it is urgently required to develop more highly effective and stable photocatalysts.[6] Metal organic frameworks (MOFs), being composed of metal (metal clusters) and organic ligands, have been widely studied in the field of gas adsorption/separation,[7] sensing,[8] and electromagnetic.[9–12] In recent years, MOFs have also emerged as strong candidate materials in the field of photocatalysis[13–18] due to their tailored structure which can give persistent porosity providing more reactive sites and tuning their light absorption properties. Nevertheless, the challenge of severe carriers’ recombination still remains to be resolved. In this regard, combining MOF materials with cocatalysts, including noble metal co-catalysts (Pt, Au, and Pd) and non-noble metal co-catalysts (transient metal, carbon, transition metal phosphide, and molybdenum sulfide), has been employed to improve their electron–hole pairs separation ability.[19–22] For example, by embedding Pt nanoparticles into the framework of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , Jiang's group successfully constructed $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}.66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ which exhibited relatively high activity for photocatalytic water splitting.[23] $\mathrm{\Pt}$ nanoparticles act as a co-catalyst impeding the electron–hole pairs recombination in MOFs, thus promoting photocatalytic water reduction. Based on the above work, they also incorporated transition metal phosphides into the MOFs and found that their performance are comparable to the noble metal Pt.[24] Apart from the above co-catalysts, MXenes (2D transition metal carbides/nitrides) also possess many advantages, including excellent conductivity, charge mobility anisotropy, and abundant surface reactive sites, and are considered as an emerging efficient co-catalyst for photocatalysis.[25–27] In recent years, they have been selected to be combined with other semiconductors to optimize their photocatalytic activity.[28,29] Therefore, MXenes as co-catalyst are potentially good options to enhance the photocatalytic activity of MOF materials.
Although the introduction of co-catalysts into MOFs appears to be a good solution to relieve electron–hole pair recombination, there remain challenges for enhancing their interactions between MOFs and co-catalysts, which is beneficial for charge transfer.[30] In previous work, the direct solvothermal strategy was often used to integrate MOFs with co-catalysts.[31] However, the high interfacial energy barrier is an obstacle to establish intimate connections between the two materials, which is unfavorable for charge transfer.[32] To solve this challenge, the surfactant synthesis method has been proposed, in which the surfactant provides docking sites for connecting two materials.[33] However, the surfactant reduces the surface area of the MOF blocking their surface reactive sites, which has a negative impact on improving their photocatalytic activity.[34] In this regard, an efficient synthesis strategy that establishes strong interactions between the MOFs and co-catalyst is required.
To construct intimately coupled MOF-based heterojunction with improved charge-carrier separation ability, we successfully construct a UiO-66 $\Join_{2}(\mathrm{Zr/Ti})$ /carboxyl-functionalized MXene (UZT/CFMX) composite using a novel and unique carboxyl group-assisted coordination route. By integrating coordination bonds, strong interactions between the two materials are formed, which is beneficial for impeding electron–hole pair recombination and adding additional ways for water reduction. Thus, the photocatalytic performance of the resulting material was enhanced significantly demonstrating ${\approx}4$ times higher performance than that of UiO-6 $\mathrm{\Omega}_{0}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}(\mathrm{Zr/Ti})$ /MXene (UZT/MX) composite without the integrated coordination bond. Moreover, the photocatalytic performance of the new material also exceeds state-of-the-art MOF-based photocatalysts. Our method provides general synthetic applicability to construct more efficient MOF/MXene hybrids for photocatalytic water splitting.
## 2. Results and Discussion
To prepare the UZT/CFMX composite, we use a novel and unique carboxyl group-assisted coordination method. As depicted in Scheme  1, by using HF as an etchant, $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}$ was firstly exfoliated into $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ nanosheets. Next, the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ nanosheets are decorated by the carboxyl-terminated diazonium salt in which the carboxyl groups coordinate to the metal cluster of the MOF acting as a bridge between the MOF and MXene. Then, through a simple solvothermal process, the UiO$66\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cdot}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{N}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ samples were uniformly attached on their surface. Last, by using the post-synthetic process, a portion of $\mathrm{zr}$ ions in $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ cluster of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is exchanged with Ti ions which generates the desired UiO-66- ${\bf\cdot N H}_{2}(\mathrm{Zr/Ti})$ /carboxyl-functionalized MXene composite.
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) tests were employed to confirm the successful preparation of the UZT, CFMX, and their composites. As shown in Figure 1b and Figure S2 (Supporting Information), the PXRD patterns of the UZT are consistent with the simulated patterns, which verifies their purity. In comparison with the simulated patterns of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}$ , the diffraction peak of the exfoliated samples at $39^{\circ}$ disappeared, indicating the elimination of Al layers.[35] Moreover, the peaks corresponding to the (002) and (004) planes of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}$ broaden and shift to lower degrees, representing the increase of lattice parameters and successful formation of the 2D $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ layers after HF etching. After decorating carboxyl groups onto the MXene, their PXRD patterns were not changed, reflecting that the grated benzoic acid has no impact on their structure. Due to the low content, no obvious peaks of CFMX are detected in the UZT/CFMX composites. The permanent porosity of the samples was analyzed by $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption tests. As shown in Figure 1c and Figures S3 and S4 (Supporting Information), the CFMX displayed no $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptakes with low Brunauer–Emmett– Teller (BET) surface area. The UZT possesses a porous structure, in which the BET surface area is about $540.2~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The BET surface area of the UZT/CFMX- $.15\%$ was calculated to be $371.9\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , lower than that of the UZT, which can be attributed to the presence of CFMX. With the increasing amount of CFMX in the composite, the BET surface area drops. The inset image in Figure  1c displays the pore size distribution of the samples, which further confirms the micropores structure of the UZT/CFMX series. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was conducted in an atmospheric environment to assess their thermal stability. As revealed in Figure 1d and Figure S5 (Supporting Information), the decomposition temperature of the UZT was about $370^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . According to the TGA results, the content of the CFMX was estimated to be $15\%$ , in accordance with the experimental results.
The morphology and microstructural features of the prepared catalysts were further verified by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images. As displayed in Figure 1e, the HF etched 2D $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ presents accordion-like multilayer structure, which is in accordance with previously reported works.[36] After grafting benzoic acid on their framework, the morphology of the CFMX was not changed, further revealing that the decorating process did not destroy their structure (Figure  1f; Figure S7, Supporting Information). From the SEM image of UZT/CFMX, it can be observed that the UZT was uniformly distributed on the interlayer surface of the CFMX (Figure  1g). On the contrary, the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ without carboxyl modification and UZT show a state of isolated growth (Figure  S9a, Supporting Information). The above results confirm that the decorated carboxyl groups play an important role in establishing an intimate connection between the two materials. The TEM images of the UZT/CFMX further demonstrate that the UZT were anchored on the surface of the layered CFMX with intimate contact (Figure S9c, Supporting Information). The enlarged high-resolution transition electron microscopy (HRTEM) image (Figure  1h) demonstrates the presence of porous UZT and CFMX. One of the adjacent lattice fringes distance is equal to ${\approx}0.25~\mathrm{nm}$ which corresponds to the (102) plane of the single-layer $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ , whereas the other one was equal to ${\approx}0.32~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is associated with the (316) plane of UZT.[37]
The images of elemental mapping demonstrated that Ti, Zr, C, and O are detected and are densely dispersed in the composite, indicating the successful formation of UZT/CFMX. The distribution of F element is not obvious, demonstrating that the HF etchant has been cleaned up in the composite (Figure 1i–n). Moreover, the content of the Ti ions in UZT have also been demonstrated. According to the inductively coupled plasma and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy spectrum (XPS) results, the proportion of $\mathrm{zr}$ and Ti can be calculated to be about 1:2, which demonstrate that the Ti ions have been successfully doped into the $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (Table S1, Supporting Information).
To demonstrate the presence of a coordination bond between UZT and CFMX, the successful decoration of the carboxyl group on $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ should be proved first. As shown in Figure S10 (Supporting Information), the absorption peak at $3200~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ corresponds to the stretching vibration of the $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups introduced on the MXene sheet after HF etching, which can be observed in MX and CFMX. Comparing the Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) spectrum before and after the carboxyl-functionalizing, a characteristic band of the carbonyl group $(1700\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}})$ can be observed in CFMX, which can be attributed to the successful decoration of carboxyl group on MXene,[38] thus forming the CFMX. Furthermore, the Raman spectra were also provided to indicate the successful decoration of carboxyl group. As presented in Figure S11 (Supporting Information), no peaks can be observed in the pristine MX. However, the peaks located at $1313\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}(D_{1}}$ bands) and $1583~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ( $\mathrm{\tilde{G}}_{1}$ bands), caused by the $\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ -hybridized carbon, are noticed in the carboxyl-functionalized MXene. The characteristic peak of CFMX for carbon species (especially of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}_{}^{}$ appears, further confirming the successful decoration of carboxyl group. Due to high charge density and bond polarization, there is a strong affinity between $\mathrm{{Zr}(I V)}$ and carboxylate O atoms in most Zr-MOFs.[39] Therefore, to establish strong interaction between $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and MXene, the carboxyl groups were predecorated on MXene. XPS analyses were performed to study the chemical states and search for more information in the UZT/CFMX composite (Figure  2a). Figure  2b shows the XPS elemental spectrum of C 1s before and after carboxyl functionalizing. The four typical carbon-containing bonds both exist in MX and CFMX but it is also evident that a new peak at $288.7\mathrm{eV}$ exists which represents the existence of a $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ bond in the CFMX.[40] The high-resolution O 1s spectrum of both MX and CFMX are shown in Figure  2c and peaks corresponding to C−O, Ti−O, and $\mathrm{Ti-OH}$ are all observed. A new peak at $533.2\mathrm{eV}$ consistent with the $\mathrm{Ti-O-C}$ bond also appeared in the CFMX,[41] which is in accordance with previous reported work. The above results further confirmed the successful decoration of carboxyl group on $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ . All elements (Ti, Zr, C, and O) can be detected in XPS spectrum of the UZT/CFMX, which correspond well to the composition of the CFMX and UZT. The high-resolution Ti 2p, $\mathrm{Zr~}3\mathrm{d}$ , and O 1s spectrum of UZT and UZT/CFMX are shown in Figure 2d–f. In the UZT/CFMX composite, the Ti $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ and Ti $2\mathrm{p}_{2/3}$ peaks are observed at 464.2 and $458.4\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively, and the $\mathrm{Zr~}3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Zr~}3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ peaks are detected at 184.9 and $182.6\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively, which exhibits a slight shift when compared with pure UZT.[42,43]
The shift of the peaks illustrates the coordination bond formed between UZT and CFMX. Moreover, the presence of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Ti-O-C}$ and $\mathsf{Z r\mathrm{-O\mathrm{-C}}}$ bonds in the UZT/CFMX composite also demonstrates the presence of a coordination bond between the two materials.[44] To further demonstrate the fact that the decorated carboxyl group contributes toward establishing the connection between UZT and CFMX, the adsorption performance of MX and CFMX for $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ ions was also investigated and compared. As shown in Figure S13 (Supporting Information), by adding MX samples into the ${\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}}$ solution, the corresponding characteristic adsorption decreased not obviously. In other words, the CFMX exhibited significantly improved ability for ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ ion adsorption demonstrating the bonding mechanism that the decorated carboxyl groups on MXene can chelate with the mixed $\mathrm{Zr/Ti}$ clusters to establish strong interaction. All of the above tests confirm that the integrated carboxyl groups build strong interactions between UZT and CFMX through coordination bonds.
Ultraviolet-visible diffuse reflectance spectra (UV−vis DRS) measurement was employed to evaluate their light harvesting performance. UZT displayed light absorption ability ranging from 200  to ${\approx}450\ \mathrm{nm}$ . In contrast, the UZT/CFMX exhibited higher light absorption intensity. Moreover, due to the presence of CFMX, their light response range is broadened to $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 3a). Based on the Tauc diagram of Kubelka–Munk equation $(\alpha h\nu=A(h\nu-\mathrm{~Eg})^{2})$ ,[45] the band gap of UZT/CFMX is equal to $2.82\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure 3b), which is roughly the same as that of UZT. The above results reflect that the presence of CFMX does not affect the band gap of the UZT. To ensure whether the UZT/CFMX possesses the capability for photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production, its conduction band (CB) position should be estimated. Mott–Schottky measurements were carried out under dark to calculate the CB of UZT at 500 and $1000~\mathrm{Hz}$ . By virtue of the positive slope, the UZT should be classified as an n-type semiconductor. Based on the Mott–Schottky results, the flat band potential $(V_{\mathrm{fb}})$ of the UZT is equal to $-0.72{\mathrm{~V~}}$ versus $\mathrm{Ag/}$ AgCl (i.e., $-0.52\mathrm{~V~}$ versus NHE). It is universally believed that the CB position of most n-type semiconductors is more negative (about 0.1 V) than $V_{\mathrm{fb}}$ . Thus, the CB of UZT was estimated to be $-0.62\mathrm{~V~}$ (Figure  3c), which is lower than the potential for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ reduction $(-0.4~\mathrm{V}$ versus NHE). Correspondingly, the valence band of UZT is found to be $2.20~\mathrm{V},$ which is more positive than the water oxidation potential.[46] The above results suggest its feasibility toward water splitting for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production.
Inspired by the above results, the photocatalytic water splitting ability of the prepared samples was examined further. Photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production experiments were carried out under simulated sunlight irradiation $(350~\mathrm{nm}<\lambda<780~\mathrm{nm})$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize5h}$ . The types of sacrificial agents are closely related to the photocatalytic activity of photocatalysts. Therefore, the influence of different sacrificial agents on their photocatalytic results was firstly investigated. As shown in the Figure S15 (Supporting Information), when TEOA was used as a sacrificial agent, it has the best performance of photocatalytic hydrogen production. The evolution amount of triethylamine is about $1603~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is slightly worse than that of triethylamine. But when sodium sulfate is used as the sacrificial agent, there is almost no hydrogen production effect. The evolution amount of UZT/CFMX under the action of other sacrificial agents is also relatively general. Therefore, in this work, the TEOA was used as sacrificial agent. Due to the low charge transformation efficiency, the photocatalytic activity of the UZT was poor without any co-catalyst. In the cases of UZT with different contents of CFMX, their photocatalytic efficiency was significantly enhanced. It is worth mentioning that the efficiency of UZT/CFMX even surpasses the Pt-loaded UZT (Figure S16a, Supporting Information), which confirmed that the CFMX is an ideal substitute for noble metal Pt to enhance the photocatalytic activity of MOF-based photocatalyst. Interestingly, the coexistence of CFMX and Pt can make a huge improvement for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution performance. Thus, photocatalytic tests were performed in the presence of Pt. Figure  3d,e displays the photocatalytic efficiency of a series of UZT/CFMX composites with a different mixing ratio $(\mathrm{UZT}/\mathrm{CFMX}{-}5\%$ , $\mathrm{UZT/CFMX}{\cdot}15\%$ , and UZT/CFMX- $25\%$ ). It is clear that the photocatalytic activity of the UZT/CFMX composites was all higher than that of pure UZT. Among them, the UZT/CFMX- $15\%$ displayed the highest photocatalytic activity for water reduction with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate as high as $2187\ \mathrm{\upmumol\h^{-1}\ g^{-1}}$ , which is approximately 20 times higher than pure UZT and also surpasses many reported MOF-based photocatalysts (Figure $3\mathrm{g}$ and Table S3, Supporting Information).[47–51] To verify their practical values, the photocatalytic water splitting tests were also performed with seawater obtained from the Yellow Sea. Under the same reaction condition, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate is $1929\ \mathrm{\upmumol\h^{-1}\ g^{-1}}$ , which is nearly the same as the result from deionized water (Figure 3f). To further verify the role of the carboxyl groups in this photocatalyst, we also combine the UZT with MX without carboxyl functionalization in UZT/MX- $15\%$ to estimate its photocatalytic activity. As expected, the photocatalytic performance of UZT/ CFMX is 4 times higher than that of UZT/MX (Figure  3d,e). The results demonstrate that the decorated carboxyl groups form strong interactions between UZT and CFMX through coordination bonds, which accelerates electron–hole pair migration/separation, thus enhancing their $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution performance. Regeneration performance is an important point for evaluating the potential practical value of the catalysts. The recycling stability of UZT/CFMX was also examined under the same reaction conditions. As shown in Figure 3h, UZT/CFMX can maintain high photocatalytic activity with small decline after four cycle tests $(20\mathrm{h})$ . Moreover, the structure of the UZT/ CFMX is maintained as demonstrated by the FTIR, PXRD, and SEM results (Figures S17 and S18, Supporting Information). All the above tests reveal the commendable practicability of the UZT/CFMX composites.
To explore the charge transportability and prove the important role of CFMX for relieving charge carriers recombination, a series of photoelectric and spectral tests were performed. The transfer dynamics of charge carriers was firstly studied by photoluminescence (PL) emission spectrum (Figure  4a). For pristine UZT, with excitation wavelength $380\mathrm{nm}$ , a broad emission peak around $470\mathrm{nm}$ is observed in the PL spectrum, which can be attributed to the intrinsic luminescence of UZT.
The PL emission peaks of UZT/MX and UZT/CFMX can both be obtained with an excitation wavelength of $380\mathrm{nm}$ . It is worth mentioning that after the addition of MX or CFMX, the intensity of the PL emission peaks is significantly decreased, which indicates that the MX or CFMX act as co-catalysts to prevent photogenerated electron–hole pairs recombination. Moreover, it can be observed that the emission peaks intensity of the UZT/CFMX is weaker than that of UZT/MX because of the presence of the carboxyl groups, which establishes the strong coordination bond between the two materials, thus further facilitating charge carriers transport and separation.[52] Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) tests were done to explore charge separation/transformation efficiency at the interface of the photocatalysts. As shown in Figure 4b, in comparison with UZT and UZT/MX, the UZT/CFMX displayed a smaller Nyquist semicircle diameter, which not only verified the important role of MXene as a co-catalyst but also further proved that the integrated coordination bond between UZT and CFMX is beneficial for charge carrier migration and separation. The important role of CFMX to improve the electrical conductivity and efficiency of charge carrier migration can also be proved by the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves. The results show that UZT/CFMX exhibited the lowest electrocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution potential, which can more efficiently convert water to produce $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (Figure 4c). The lifetime of the electron–hole pairs for the composite photocatalysts was then tested by time-resolved fluorescence spectra (Figure 4d). In comparison with UZT/MX, whose lifetime is ${\approx}1.51$ ns, the UZT/CFMX displayed a smaller lifetime. This result proves the important role of decorated carboxyl groups for facilitating charge transfer between the two materials.[53] Transient photocurrent response was examined to unveil the charge separation efficiencies of UZT, UZT/ MX, and UZT/CFMX. As shown in Figure 4e, the intensity of photocurrent exhibits the following order: UZT $<\mathrm{UZT/MX}<$ UZT/CFMX, which is in accordance with the photocatalytic results, revealing that the intimate connections between UZT and CFMX contribute significantly to photogenerated electron–hole pair separation. Apart from these results, the higher surface photovoltage response intensity (Figure  4f) of UZT/ CFMX also demonstrates the positive effects of the coordination bonds between UZT and CFMX. It is surprising that all kinetic data are consistent with the results of photocatalytic hydrogen production activity.
To identify the important role of the carboxyl group and photocatalytic mechanism, density functional theory (DFT) calculations were performed by using the VASP software package with the projected augmented wave (PAW) method.[54,55] The optimized structures of UZT/MX and UZT/CFMX are displayed in Figure ${4\mathrm{g}}$ and Figure S19 (Supporting Information). The MOF locates above the MXene and does not form any bond with the MXene. However, upon carboxyl-functionalization, the O atom from the carboxyl-functionalized MXene forms a bond with the $\mathrm{zr}$ atom in the MOF, establishing strong bonds between UZT and CFMX. The hybridization at the Fermi level between the d orbital of $\mathrm{zr}$ and p orbital of O from the projected density of states (PDOS) analysis of UZT/CFMX in Figure  4h also confirms the formation of bonds between $\mathrm{zr}$ and O. The electron density difference analysis of UZT/CFMX indicates that the O atom receives electrons whereas Zr loses electrons (Figure S20, Supporting Information). In addition, the electron density differences are larger for UZT/CFMX than that of UZT/MX. Moreover, the electron density difference is mainly located around the benzoic acid part, indicating that carboxyl-functionalization plays an important role in connecting UZT and MX. Interestingly, based on band structure analysis (Figure  4i), the band number near the Fermi level are greater for the UZT/CFMX than UZT/MX, implying that presence of benzoic acid can not only help to construct a coordination bond between UZT and CFMX but can also promote electron transfer.
UZT/CFMX case $2>$ UZT/CFMX case $1>\mathrm{UZT/MX}$ , further confirming that the incorporation of benzoic acid promotes the water splitting reaction consistent with experimental results (Figure 5a). Overall, the breakage of the $_\mathrm{H-O}$ bond as well as the larger adsorption energy both suggests that UZT/CFMX promotes water splitting, corresponding well with experimental results explaining the higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate observed for UZT/CFMX.
Based on the PDOS analysis, no obvious hybridization peaks exist near the Fermi level between the s orbital of H and $\mathtt{p}$ orbital of O for water adsorption on UZT/MX (Figure  5b), indicating that H from water does not form a bond with O at the surface of MXene. With regards to water adsorption on UZT/CFMX Cases 1 and 2, obvious hybridization peaks near the Fermi level between the s orbital of H and p orbital of O implies the formation of an $_\mathrm{O-H}$ bond (Figure  5c,d). Furthermore, the overlap between the p orbital of C and O suggests the formation of a $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C-O}$ bond, confirming that the existence of benzoic acid promotes water decomposition (Figure  5e,f). In addition, the electron density differences for UZT/CFMX Cases 1 and 2 are more obvious than that of UZT/MX, corresponding well with the above analysis and further confirming that the interactions between UZT/CFMX and water are much stronger than UZT/MX (Figure 5g,i).
Accordingly, a photocatalytic water splitting mechanism of the UZT/CFMX is proposed (Scheme 2). After light irradiation, the electron is excited and transfers from the VB of UZT to the CB. Next, the benzoic acid on MXene acts as a “bridge” promoting the transfer of electrons to the surface of CFMX. Thereafter, electrons migrate to the surface reactive sites and participate in the reaction of water reduction. The benzoic acid promotes the water splitting reaction via three aspects. First, it helps the formation of UZT/CFMX. When MXene was carboxyl-functionalized, the O atom from benzoic acid directly forms a bond with the $\mathrm{zr}$ atom from MOF, making MOF and MXene bond strongly together. Second, based on the band structure analysis, benzoic acid promotes electron transfer, thus enhancing the photocatalytic reaction leading to higher hydrogen production rate. Third, the carbon atom from the carboxyl group forms a bond with the oxygen from water adding two additional reaction pathways to water splitting making it easier for water to dissociate. Consequently, the resulting UZT/CFMX composite can effectively trigger water splitting with high performance.
## 3. Conclusions
In summary, we have proposed a unique carboxyl groupassisted coordination route to successfully construct a
UZT/CFMX composite photocatalyst. Through a series of tests, we demonstrate that the decorated carboxyl group establishes strong coordination bonds between the two materials, thus facilitating charge carrier migration/separation. In addition, an interesting result is observed that the carbon atom on the carboxyl group forms a bond with the oxygen from water, thus adding two additional reaction pathways for water splitting, and making water dissociation easier. By virtue of above advantages, the formed UZT/CFMX composites exhibit high photocatalytic performance for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production. The $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate reaches up to $2187\ \mathrm{\upmumol\g^{-1}\ h^{-1}};$ 20 times higher than that of UZT and 4 times higher than that of $\mathrm{UZT/MX}$ , which also exceeds most reported MOFs based photocatalysts. This work provides a unique strategy for establishing intimately coupled MOF-based heterojunctions to enhance water splitting performance.
## 4. Experimental Section
Preparation of UiO- $56\small-N H_{2}(Z r/T i)$ (UZT): The UiO- $66\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}(\mathsf{Z r}/\mathsf{T i})$ was prepared according to the reported method with modifications.[23,24,42] At first, $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF solution with $Z r C l_{4}$ $(40.8~\mathrm{mg},0.775~\mathrm{mmol},$ ) and $5~\mathsf{m L}$ of DMF solution with 2-amino-1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $(\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -BDC) $(58~\mathrm{mg},0.32~\mathrm{mmol})$ were mixed together in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ glass vial. Following that, adding $0.6~\mathsf{m L}$ of acetic acid and $0.6~\mathsf{m L}$ of formic acid, and the solution was sealed and heated at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The product was collected by centrifugation at 10  000  rpm for $5\mathrm{\min}$ and washed with DMF several times, and then immersed in methanol for 3 days. Then, $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}(Z\mathsf{r})$ was activated by removing the solvent under vacuum for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ .  After  that, $30~\mathsf{m g}$ of the collected $N H_{2}{\cdot}\cup i0{\cdot}66(Z r)$ in $3m L$ DMF, $33~\mathsf{m g}~{\mathsf{T i C l}}_{4}({\mathsf{T H F}})_{2}$ in $3m L$ DMF were mixed in a $70~\mathsf{m L}$ glass vial. The mixture was heated at ${\mathsf{120}}\ {\mathsf{^{o}C}}$ for $\rceil8\mathrm{~h~}$ . The product was collected by centrifugation at 10  000  rpm for $5\mathrm{\min}$ and washed with methanol several times. The solids were dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in a vacuum for $12\textmd{h}$ to get Ti exchanged UiO-66- $\mathsf{\cdot N H}_{2}(\mathsf{Z r}/\mathsf{T i})$ (denoted as UZT).
Preparation of Carboxyl-Functionalized MXene(CFMX): The carboxylfunctionalized MXene was prepared according to the reported method with small changes.[56] To prepare the carboxyl-functionalized MXene, the phenylcarboxylic acid diazonium salt was firstly synthesized through the following steps: 7  mmol of 4-aminobenzoic acid $(960~\mathsf{m g})$ and 7  mmol of sodium hydroxide $(280~\mathsf{m g})$ were mixed in $80~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water. Following that, 7.6  mmol of sodium nitrite $(526~\mathsf{m g})$ was slowly added into the solution and kept at $0{-}5~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . Then, $3.5~\mathsf{m L}$ of HCl solution ( $34-36\%$ , $12\textsf{m}$ , 19.2  mmol) was poured into the mixture and stirred for $45\mathrm{\min}$ to yield a pale yellow product. After that, around $80~\mathrm{mL}$ diazonium salt solution and $70~\mathrm{mL}$ of MXene colloidal solution $(70\ m g\ m L^{-1})$ were mixed together and stirred at room temperature for $4\ h$ . The obtained powders were separated and washed with water, acetone, and ethanol successively to remove extra salts (5 min each time, $10000~\mathsf{r p m})$ ). Finally, the black product (CFMX) was dried under vacuum at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ overnight.
Preparation of UiO- $.66\substack{-N H_{2}(Z r/T i)}$ /Carboxyl-Functionalized MXene (UZT/CFMX): A series of UZT/CFMX composites with different ratios (CFMX: UZT $=5\%$ , $15\%$ , and $25\%$ ) were successfully synthesized through a simple solvothermal and followed by post-synthetic method. Typically, 5, 15, or $25~{\mathsf{m g}}$ of CFMX, $5~\mathsf{m L}$ of DMF solution with $Z r C l_{4}$ $(40.8~\mathrm{mg},~0.775~\mathrm{mmol})$ , and $5m L$ of DMF solution with 2-amino-1,4- benzenedicarboxylic acid $(N H_{2}\cdot B D C)$ $(58~\mathrm{mg},0.32~\mathrm{mmol})$ were mixed together in a $20~\mathsf{m L}$ glass vial. Following that, adding $0.6~\mathsf{m L}$ of acetic acid and $0.6~\mathsf{m L}$ of formic acid, and the solution was sealed and heated at ${\mathsf{120}}\ {\mathsf{^{o}C}}$ for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The product was collected by centrifugation at $70\ 000$ rpm for $5\mathrm{\min}$ and washed with DMF for several times, and then immersed in methanol for 3 days. Then, UiO-66- ${\sf N H}_{2}(Z{\sf r})/$ carboxyl-functionalized MXene with different ratios were activated by removing the solvent under vacuum for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . After that, $30~\mathsf{m g}$ of collected $\mathsf{U i O-66-N H}_{2}(Z\mathsf{r})/$ carboxyl-functionalized MXene in $3m L$ DMF, 33  mg ${\mathsf{T i C l}}_{4}({\mathsf{T H F}})_{2}$ in $3m L$ DMF were mixed in a $70~\mathsf{m L}$ glass vial. The mixture was heated at ${\mathsf{120}}\ {\mathsf{^{o}C}}$ for $\rceil8\mathrm{~h~}$ . The product was collected by centrifugation at 10  000  rpm for 5  min and washed with methanol several times. The solids were dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in a vacuum for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ to get Ti exchanged $\mathsf{U i O-66-N H_{2}(Z r/T i)}$ /carboxyl-functionalized MXene (denoted as UZT/CFMX- $5\%$ , UZT/CFMX- $15\%$ , UZT/CFMX- $25\%$ , respectively).
Characterization: All X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were obtained using a Bruker (Germany) D8 Advance diffractometer with $\mathsf{C u}\ \mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation from $5^{\circ}$ to $65^{\circ}$ at $30~\mathsf{k V}$ and $70~\mathsf{m A}.$ The surface morphology and structure of the samples were characterized by using SEM (Japan, $\mathsf{S u8010}_{\mathsf{\Pi}}^{\mathsf{1}}$ ) at $5~\mathsf{k V}$ and TEM (Japan, JEM-2100). EDS maps of the materials were obtained using a scanning transmission electron microscope (STEM) equipped with an EDS detector (JEOL 2100F). The BET specific surface areas and pore volume were obtained by ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption instrument (QuantachromeAsiQ-C, American) at $77~\mathsf{K}.$ TGA (METTLER TOLEDO TGA/SDTA851) were performed to analyze the structure with a heating rate of $5~{}^{\circ}C~\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ under air atmosphere. FTIR was performed on a Japan Shimadzu Spectrum IR-Prestige-21 using KBr pellets. The contents of Ti and Zr in the UZT were quantified by an Agilent ICP-OES 725 ES inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES). XPS measurements were carried out on an England Kratos, Axis UltraDLD spectrometer using Al $\mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation at $\mathsf{75~k V}$ with $160~\mathrm{eV}$ pass energy. PL spectra were recorded using an American Varian Cary Eclipse at room temperature with $380\ n m$ excitation. Time-resolved photoluminescence spectroscopy (TRPL) was recorded on a PMT900 instrument at room temperature; the excitation wavelength is $375\ \mathsf{n m}$ . Raman spectra were collected on a LabRAMHR Evolution Raman microscope. UV–vis DRS absorption spectra were recorded using a Shimadzu UV-2550 spectrophotometer with ${\mathsf{B a S O}}_{4}$ as a reflectance standard.
Photocatalytic Hydrogen Production Tests: Photocatalytic hydrogen production reaction was carried out in a Pyrex glass reactor equipped with an all gas automatic on-line trace gas analysis system (Beijing Perfectlight Technology Co., Ltd., Labsolar-6A, China). A 300 W simulated sunlight irradiation Xe lamp $(350~\mathsf{n m}<\lambda<780~\mathsf{n m})$ was used as the light source. Place the Xenon lamp source $20\ c m$ from the upper area of the solution. In a photocatalytic hydrogen production experiment, the as-prepared photocatalysts (to the nearest $\ 10\ m\ g)$ ) were dispersed in an aqueous solution $(700~\mathsf{m L})$ ) containing $70\ \mathrm{\vol\%}$ triethanolamine and $400\ \upmu\mathsf{L}\ \mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{P t C l}_{6}$ $(70\times70^{-3}\textbf{M})$ . Before the reaction irradiation, the reaction system was maintained at $5^{\circ}C$ by circulating cooling water, and then vacuum until the indicated pressure of the system reaches the minimum value which the air is completely expelled. The hydrogen collected in the reaction system was automatically analyzed using a gas chromatograph equipped with a 5 A molecular sieve column and thermal conductivity detector (TCD). During the entire reaction, nitrogen was used as the carrier gas for the chromatography.
